issue.body:
**Kibana version:** 8.10.2

**Elasticsearch version:** 8.10.2

**Server OS version:** Docker Desktop 4.22.1

**Browser version:** Chromium: 117.0.5938.140

**Browser OS version:** MacOS 13.6

**Original install method (e.g. download page, yum, from source, etc.):** official docker image

**Describe the bug:**

Creation of a runtime field for a TSDS data view fails during preview, but succeed while forced with the API.

**Steps to reproduce:**

1. Execute the below commands in dev tools to create a test TSDS and a document, replace the TIMESTAMP with something current so that the document is not rejected (because of TSDS lookahead):

```
PUT /_ilm/policy/metrics-monitoring-logstash
{
  "policy": {
    "phases": {
      "hot": {
        "min_age": "0ms",
        "actions": {
          "rollover": {
            "max_age": "30d",
            "max_primary_shard_size": "50gb"
          }
        }
      },
      "warm": {
        "min_age": "0d",
        "actions": {
          "downsample": {
            "fixed_interval": "30s"
          },
          "forcemerge": {
            "max_num_segments": 1,
            "index_codec": "best_compression"
          }
        }
      },
      "cold": {
        "min_age": "7d",
        "actions": {
          "downsample": {
            "fixed_interval": "5m"
          }
        }
      },
      "delete": {
        "min_age": "90d",
        "actions": {
          "delete": {
            "delete_searchable_snapshot": true
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}


PUT /_index_template/metrics-monitoring-logstash
{
  "index_patterns": [
    "metrics-logstash.monitoring-*"
  ],
  "data_stream": {},
  "priority": 1000,
  "template": {
    "mappings": {
      "dynamic_templates": [
        {
          "strings_as_keyword": {
            "mapping": {
              "ignore_above": 1024,
              "type": "keyword"
            },
            "match_mapping_type": "string"
          }
        }
      ],
      "_source": {
        "mode": "synthetic"
      },
      "properties": {
        "@timestamp": {
          "type": "date"
        },
        "event.ingested": {
          "type": "date"
        },
        "logstash": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "uuid": {
              "type": "keyword",
              "time_series_dimension": true
            },
            "name": {
              "type": "keyword"
            }
          }
        },
        "pipeline.id": {
          "type": "keyword",
          "time_series_dimension": true
        },
        "events": {
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "in": {
              "type": "long",
              "time_series_metric": "counter"
            },
            "filtered": {
              "type": "long",
              "time_series_metric": "counter"
            },
            "out": {
              "type": "long",
              "time_series_metric": "counter"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "settings": {
      "number_of_shards": 1,
      "number_of_replicas": 1,
      "index": {
        "mode": "time_series",
        "lifecycle.name": "metrics-monitoring-logstash",
        "look_ahead_time": "1h"
      }
    }
  }
}

POST metrics-logstash.monitoring-default/_doc
{
  "@timestamp": "TIMESTAMP",
  "pipeline.id": "foobar",
  "logstash.uuid": "qwerty",
  "logstash.name": "logstash_myhostprefix",
  "events.in": 10,
  "events.filtered": 10,
  "events.out": 10
}
```

3. Create a dataview for `metrics-logstash.monitoring-default`.
4. If you attempt to create a runtime field, you will be met with the below error:

```
_id must be unset or set to [GvMPW29fWPE5Sr2ZAAABivq9G6A] but was [_id] because [.ds-metrics-logstash.monitoring-default-2023.10.04-000001] is in time_series mode
```

This seems to be ultimately caused by [this piece of code](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/main/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/index/mapper/TsidExtractingIdFieldMapper.java#L130-L137)

5. Where it gets interesting is that creating the field through Kibana APIs works fine, and the field can be consumed in discover:

```
POST kbn:/api/data_views/data_view/DATA_VIEW_ID/runtime_field
{
  "name": "foo",
  "runtimeField": {
     "type": "keyword",
     "script": {
        "source": "emit('foobar');"
      }
  }
}

```

6. After creating the runtime field with the API, I could confirm things work normally when exploring data (or querying) in discover.

**Expected behavior:**

I'd expect any runtime field to work on TSDS, provided that I don't access `_source` (because of synthetics). The validation performed in the interface fails for seemingly no good reason, since things work just fine when it's bypassed with the API.
Another question would also be "why is the same validation not performed with the API", but it's a different problem :)

